  "result": "The quote now prints **27.06**; before the fix it printed 26.09.\n\n**Root cause:** the bug was in `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9`. It padded the decimal part on the wrong side: `\"5\".rjust(2, \"0\")` gives `\"05\"`, so `\"12.5\"` was read as 1205 cents ($12.05) instead of 1250. Only prices with one decimal place were affected; whole numbers and two-decimal prices were already correct.\n\n**Fix:** I changed `rjust` to `ljust`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. Checks after the fix:\n- `\"12.5\"` \u2192 1250, `\"12.05\"` \u2192 1205, `\"12\"` \u2192 1200, `\"0.5\"` \u2192 50.\n- The quote works out as 1250 \u00d7 2 = 2500, plus 8.25% tax (206) = 2706, which prints as 27.06.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest` and all 6 pass. None of them use a one-decimal price, which is how this bug got through. I didn't add a test for `to_cents`.",
